1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,089
Lesson 9.5.

2
00:00:02,469 --> 00:00:05,812
Efficiency analysis: Route A vs Route B.

3
00:00:06,917 --> 00:00:10,523
If we want to generate with thermodynamic
hardware, there are two paths.

4
00:00:10,903 --> 00:00:15,743
Route A takes an existing neural
diffusion model, such as a diffusion

5
00:00:15,743 --> 00:00:21,005
transformer or a masked language model,
and ports it to a system with TSUs.

6
00:00:21,385 --> 00:00:24,238
Route B designs a native
model from scratch,

7
00:00:24,238 --> 00:00:26,962
such as a DTM, that lives in the hardware.

8
00:00:27,342 --> 00:00:28,971
Today we compare them with numbers.

9
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:34,021
The advantage of route A is that
it reuses models that already work.

10
00:00:34,401 --> 00:00:38,002
The problem is that a
transformer is mostly matrix

11
00:00:38,002 --> 00:00:42,755
multiplications, and a TSU does
not multiply matrices: it samples.

12
00:00:43,135 --> 00:00:47,277
The deterministic parts stay
in digital logic, an FPGA or

13
00:00:47,277 --> 00:00:50,983
an accelerator, and the
TSU only does the sampling.

14
00:00:51,363 --> 00:00:56,938
In the figure Extropic reports for its
Z1T model, of two hundred ninety-four

15
00:00:56,938 --> 00:01:02,879
nanojoules per token, two hundred eighty-six
are FPGA work: ninety-seven percent.

16
00:01:03,983 --> 00:01:05,851
Amdahl's law sets the limit.

17
00:01:06,231 --> 00:01:10,364
If ninety-seven percent of the
energy is digital glue, making the

18
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:14,687
thermodynamic part free improves
the total by at most three percent.

19
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:18,751
To approach thousand-fold
gains one must attack the glue:

20
00:01:18,751 --> 00:01:22,500
move more functions onto the
probabilistic chip, lower the

21
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,796
digital precision or change
the model architecture.

22
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:31,528
Route B reverses the logic: the
whole model is designed as a chain of

23
00:01:31,528 --> 00:01:35,605
conditional energy models that
the hardware samples directly.

24
00:01:35,985 --> 00:01:40,342
There is almost no glue: in the
DTM of lesson 9.3, the energy

25
00:01:40,342 --> 00:01:44,342
is that of the cells, about
twenty nanojoules per image.

26
00:01:44,722 --> 00:01:49,483
The price is expressivity: these
models are more limited, and large

27
00:01:49,483 --> 00:01:54,245
datasets need hybrids with a neural
network, which reintroduce part

28
00:01:54,245 --> 00:01:58,012
of the glue, though with a
network ten times smaller.

29
00:01:59,117 --> 00:02:00,063
Let's compare them.

30
00:02:00,443 --> 00:02:04,087
Route A has mature models
and known quality, but

31
00:02:04,087 --> 00:02:07,656
its energy gain is limited
by the digital glue.

32
00:02:08,036 --> 00:02:13,313
Route B has a potential energy advantage
of orders of magnitude, but with

33
00:02:13,313 --> 00:02:17,867
young models, small benchmarks
and estimates, not measurements.

34
00:02:18,247 --> 00:02:20,943
Neither is better at
everything: it depends on

35
00:02:20,943 --> 00:02:23,522
whether the bottleneck
is quality or energy.

36
00:02:24,650 --> 00:02:26,029
A checklist for deciding.

37
00:02:26,409 --> 00:02:30,530
First, measure the digital-glue
fraction of route A: if it

38
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:34,936
exceeds ninety percent, the
maximum gain is ten times or less.

39
00:02:35,316 --> 00:02:40,130
Second, ask whether a native model of
sufficient quality exists for your task.

40
00:02:40,510 --> 00:02:44,549
Third, count all the energy,
including input and output.

41
00:02:44,929 --> 00:02:49,818
And fourth, label each figure as measured,
simulated, projected or estimated.

42
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:54,874
This analysis helps plan products,
steer research toward native

43
00:02:54,874 --> 00:02:58,798
models of sufficient quality and
decide which functions to move

44
00:02:58,798 --> 00:03:01,975
onto the chip, as we will
see in unit ten with Z1T.

45
00:03:02,355 --> 00:03:06,389
In project eight you will build a
heterogeneous inference planner.

46
00:03:07,517 --> 00:03:11,784
Common mistakes: quoting the
TSU's energy as the system's.

47
00:03:12,164 --> 00:03:16,203
Comparing a native model with a
GPU one at different qualities.

48
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:19,302
Assuming hybrids have no digital cost.

49
00:03:19,682 --> 00:03:21,851
And treating projections as measurements.

50
00:03:22,983 --> 00:03:27,361
In summary: route A ports mature
models but is limited by the

51
00:03:27,361 --> 00:03:32,026
digital glue, which in Z1T is
ninety-seven percent of the energy.

52
00:03:32,406 --> 00:03:37,296
Route B, native, promises orders of
magnitude with models that are still young.

53
00:03:37,676 --> 00:03:41,660
Amdahl's law and complete
accounting decide in each case.

54
00:03:42,783 --> 00:03:46,815
To practice: compute
the FPGA share in Z1T.

55
00:03:47,195 --> 00:03:50,448
Compute the maximum gain
if the TSU were free.

56
00:03:50,828 --> 00:03:53,442
Say what must change to
approach a thousand times.

57
00:03:53,822 --> 00:03:56,359
And explain when you would
still choose route A.
